            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA reported on Aug. 5, 2026, that Perseverance captured a first-of-its-kind observation: Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos as seen from the surface of another planet. The rover used its Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026 — sol 1,907 of the mission — to image Earth as a tiny bright dot being occulted by Phobos in the Martian evening sky【6:1†source】.\n\nOther recent updates:\n\n- Perseverance is still active and sending back images; NASA’s raw-image page shows “Image of the Week” selections continuing through Week 285, covering July 26–Aug. 1, 2026【6:0†source】.\n- In July 2026, NASA highlighted that Perseverance had driven the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — reaching that distance on sol 1,890 after about five years and four months of surface operations【6:4†source】.\n- The rover was operating west of Jezero Crater in a region nicknamed “Arbot” when Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter imagery showed it and its tracks from orbit【6:4†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance remains operational, recently passed a major driving milestone, and just produced a unique “Earth behind Phobos” observation from the Martian surface.",
